---
/**
 * A world map of points that pans and zooms, with no JavaScript required. The
 * one script at the foot of this file adds mouse controls on top; see MOUSE
 * CONTROLS below.
 *
 * Panning is native scrolling. The map is drawn larger than its frame and the
 * frame scrolls: a finger drags it, a trackpad pans it in both axes, a mouse
 * wheel scrolls it and shift-wheel scrolls it sideways, the scrollbars can be
 * dragged, and the arrow keys move it once the frame has focus. Nothing had to
 * be written for any of that.
 *
 * Zoom is a radio group. Each level sets the width of the map to a multiple of
 * the frame, and the step controls are labels for the level either side.
 *
 * The hard part is where a zoom lands. A scroll offset is a pixel count, so
 * doubling the width under an unchanged offset slides the view toward the top
 * left corner of the world, and every zoom would throw the reader somewhere
 * they were not looking. The fix is scroll snapping. The map carries a lattice
 * of invisible snap lines, one set of columns and one of rows, spread evenly by
 * percentage so they scale with the map. When the layout changes, a browser
 * re-snaps the frame to the SAME snap target it was on, and the same target is
 * the same place on the map at any width. So the point in the middle of the
 * frame stays in the middle through every zoom.
 *
 * Three details make that hold, each found by testing it in Chromium:
 *
 *  - The lines span the whole map in the other axis. Chromium only snaps an
 *    axis to an area that is visible in the other axis at the proposed
 *    position, so a one-pixel column marker at the top of the map stops being
 *    a candidate the moment the frame scrolls down, and nothing snaps at all.
 *  - At the widest view the map fits the frame and there is nothing to scroll,
 *    so the browser snaps to whichever target is nearest offset zero, which is
 *    the top left corner, and the first zoom from there went to Alaska. At
 *    that level only the centre lines are snap targets, so the first zoom
 *    lands on the middle of the map.
 *  - The lattice is dense enough that snapping reads as panning. 768 columns
 *    across a map sixteen frames wide is one line every 27px on a laptop.
 *
 * A browser that does not re-snap to the same target after a layout change
 * falls back to the nearest line to the old offset, which is the top-left
 * drift described above and nothing worse.
 *
 * Markers are HTML, not SVG. An SVG circle scales with the map and would be
 * sixteen times the size at the deepest zoom; an element stays the size it was
 * drawn at. They are placed with margins, not with position or transform,
 * because either of those would make the marker the containing block of its
 * preview card, and a card contained by a marker inside the scrolling frame is
 * clipped by the frame. With margins the card's containing block is the frame's
 * positioned parent, outside the scroller, so it is never cut off.
 *
 * Every marker is a button that opens its card as a native popover, which is
 * the route on a touch screen. Hovering shows the same card without opening it.
 *
 * MOUSE CONTROLS, the site's one first-party script. On the owner's instruction
 * the wheel zooms and a mouse drag pans, which no CSS can do: a mouse cannot
 * drag-scroll natively, and a wheel event cannot drive a zoom. Everything above
 * still works without it and is the baseline; the script only drives the same
 * radio group and the same scroller.
 *
 *  - Wheel: one level per notch, by checking the neighbouring radio, then
 *    scrolling so the point under the pointer stays under it. Deltas are
 *    accumulated and levels rate-limited, because a trackpad sends a stream of
 *    small deltas that would otherwise run straight to the last level. At the
 *    widest level a wheel outwards, and at the deepest a wheel inwards, is left
 *    to the page, so scrolling past the map is not trapped. A mostly
 *    horizontal wheel is left alone and pans natively.
 *  - Drag: mouse only, and only when there is something to scroll. Snapping is
 *    off while dragging, or every move lands on the lattice and at 16x the map
 *    jumps in 27px steps; releasing snaps to the nearest line. A drag that ends
 *    on a marker does not open its card.
 *
 * It is inline and identical wherever the map renders, so the Content-Security-
 * Policy in public/_headers allows it by its SHA-256 hash and allows no other
 * script of ours. check:built recomputes the hash from dist and fails if the
 * policy does not carry it: edit the script, rebuild, and copy the new hash
 * that check prints into public/_headers.
 */
import { projectLand } from "../lib/geo";

export interface ZoomPoint {
  lon: number;
  lat: number;
  /** Marker diameter in CSS pixels, chosen by the caller from the data. */
  d: number;
  /**
   * How much of the marker is filled, as a fraction of its diameter: 1 is a
   * solid disc, 0 an empty ring, anything between a disc inside a ring. Lets
   * one marker carry two magnitudes of the same thing, such as capacity now
   * inside capacity planned.
   */
  fill?: number;
  /** Drawn in the muted ink, for a point whose size is not known. */
  muted?: boolean;
  /** Filter keys this point belongs to. Unchecking any of them hides it. */
  tags?: string[];
  /** Accessible name of the marker. */
  label: string;
  /** Card heading. */
  name: string;
  /** Line under the heading. */
  sub?: string;
  /** Card rows, label then value. */
  rows: Array<[string, string]>;
  /** Link from the card, usually to the point's row in a table. */
  href?: string;
  hrefLabel?: string;
}

export interface ZoomFilter {
  key: string;
  label: string;
  checked?: boolean;
}

interface Props {
  points: ZoomPoint[];
  /** Accessible description of what the map shows. */
  label: string;
  /** Unique per page, and becomes both an HTML id and a CSS selector. */
  id?: string;
  /** Zoom multiples, widest first. */
  levels?: number[];
  filters?: ZoomFilter[];
}

const { points, label, id = "zm", levels = [1, 2, 4, 8, 16], filters = [] } = Astro.props;

/* `id` and each filter key are interpolated into a raw <style> block via
   set:html, as selectors. Anything that is not a plain identifier fails the
   build rather than being rewritten, the same rule as TimeMap's labels. */
const IDENT = /^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]{0,31}$/;
if (!IDENT.test(id)) {
  throw new Error(`ZoomMap: id ${JSON.stringify(id)} is not a plain identifier.`);
}
for (const filter of filters) {
  if (!IDENT.test(filter.key)) {
    throw new Error(`ZoomMap: filter key ${JSON.stringify(filter.key)} is not a plain identifier.`);
  }
}
if (!levels.length || levels[0] !== 1 || levels.some((z, i) => i > 0 && z <= levels[i - 1])) {
  throw new Error(`ZoomMap: levels must start at 1 and rise, got ${JSON.stringify(levels)}.`);
}

const world = projectLand(2000, 1);

/* Largest first, so a small marker is never painted underneath a large one it
   sits inside and can always be reached by the pointer. */
const placed = points
  .map((point) => ({ point, at: world.place(point.lon, point.lat) }))
  .filter((p): p is { point: ZoomPoint; at: [number, number] } => p.at !== null)
  .sort((a, b) => b.point.d - a.point.d);

/* A point the projection cannot place would vanish from the map and from
   nothing else, so the table below it would disagree with the picture. */
if (placed.length !== points.length) {
  const lost = points.filter((p) => !world.place(p.lon, p.lat)).map((p) => p.name);
  throw new Error(`ZoomMap: ${lost.length} point(s) could not be projected: ${lost.join(", ")}`);
}

/* The snap lattice. Even counts, so the middle falls between two lines and the
   pair either side of it are both marked as the centre. */
const COLS = 768;
const ROWS = 384;
const midCol = COLS / 2 - 1;
const midRow = ROWS / 2 - 1;

const pct = (n: number) => n.toFixed(3);

const rules = [
  ...levels.map(
    (z, i) => `#${id}:has(#${id}-z${i}:checked) .zmap__canvas { --z: ${z}; }
#${id}:has(#${id}-z${i}:checked) .zmap__step[data-from="${i}"] { display: inline-flex; }
#${id}:has(#${id}-z${i}:checked) .zmap__level::after { content: "${z}\\00D7"; }
#${id}:has(#${id}-z${i}:focus-visible) .zmap__zoom { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 2px; }`,
  ),
  /* At the widest level there is nothing to scroll, and only the centre lines
     are targets. See the header. */
  `#${id}:has(#${id}-z0:checked) .zmap__cols > i:not(.is-mid),
#${id}:has(#${id}-z0:checked) .zmap__rows > i:not(.is-mid) { scroll-snap-align: none; }`,
  `#${id}:has(#${id}-z0:checked) .zmap__step--home { opacity: 0.45; pointer-events: none; }`,
  /* A grab cursor only where a drag can move something, and the drag state the
     mouse script sets. Exclusive of each other, so neither has to win on
     specificity. */
  `#${id}:not(:has(#${id}-z0:checked)):not(.is-dragging) .zmap__view { cursor: grab; }`,
  `#${id}.is-dragging .zmap__view { cursor: grabbing; scroll-snap-type: none; user-select: none; }`,
  ...filters.map(
    (f) => `#${id}:has(#${id}-f-${f.key}:not(:checked)) .zmap__site.t-${f.key} { display: none; }`,
  ),
].join("\n");
---

<div class="zmap" id={id}>
  <div class="zmap__radios" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Zoom">
    {
      levels.map((z, i) => (
        <input
          class="zmap__radio"
          type="radio"
          name={`${id}-zoom`}
          id={`${id}-z${i}`}
          checked={i === 0}
          aria-label={z === 1 ? "Whole world" : `Zoom ${z} times`}
        />
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <div class="zmap__frame">
    <div class="zmap__view" tabindex="0" role="group" aria-label={`${label}. Scrolls in both directions when zoomed.`}>
      <div class="zmap__canvas">
        <svg viewBox={`0 0 ${world.width} ${world.height}`} class="zmap__svg" role="img" aria-label={label}>
          <path d={world.land} class="zmap__land"></path>
          <path d={world.borders} class="zmap__borders"></path>
        </svg>

        <span class="zmap__cols" aria-hidden="true">
          {Array.from({ length: COLS }, (_, i) => <i class:list={[(i === midCol || i === midCol + 1) && "is-mid"]} />)}
        </span>
        <span class="zmap__rows" aria-hidden="true">
          {Array.from({ length: ROWS }, (_, i) => <i class:list={[(i === midRow || i === midRow + 1) && "is-mid"]} />)}
        </span>

        {
          placed.map(({ point, at: [x, y] }, i) => {
            const card = `${id}-c${i}`;
            const anchor = `--${id}-a${i}`;
            return (
              <div
                class:list={["zmap__site", ...(point.tags ?? []).map((t) => `t-${t}`)]}
                style={`--x:${pct(x)};--y:${pct(y)};--d:${point.d.toFixed(1)}px;--f:${Math.min(1, Math.max(0, point.fill ?? 1)).toFixed(3)};anchor-name:${anchor}`}
              >
                <button
                  type="button"
                  class:list={["zmap__dot", point.muted && "is-muted"]}
                  popovertarget={card}
                  tabindex="-1"
                  aria-label={point.label}
                />
                <div popover id={card} class="zmap__card" style={`position-anchor:${anchor}`}>
                  <span class="zmap__name">{point.name}</span>
                  {point.sub && <span class="zmap__sub">{point.sub}</span>}
                  <dl class="zmap__rows-list">
                    {point.rows.map(([k, v]) => (
                      <div class="zmap__row">
                        <dt>{k}</dt>
                        <dd>{v}</dd>
                      </div>
                    ))}
                  </dl>
                  {point.href && (
                    <a class="zmap__more" href={point.href}>
                      {point.hrefLabel ?? "Details"}
                    </a>
                  )}
                </div>
              </div>
            );
          })
        }
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="zmap__controls">
      <span class="zmap__zoom">
        {
          levels.map((_, i) =>
            i === 0 ? (
              <span class="zmap__step zmap__step--off" data-from="0" aria-hidden="true">
                &minus;
              </span>
            ) : (
              <label class="zmap__step" data-from={String(i)} for={`${id}-z${i - 1}`} aria-label="Zoom out">
                &minus;
              </label>
            ),
          )
        }
        <span class="zmap__level mono" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        {
          levels.map((_, i) =>
            i === levels.length - 1 ? (
              <span class="zmap__step zmap__step--off" data-from={String(i)} aria-hidden="true">
                +
              </span>
            ) : (
              <label class="zmap__step" data-from={String(i)} for={`${id}-z${i + 1}`} aria-label="Zoom in">
                +
              </label>
            ),
          )
        }
      </span>
      <label class="zmap__step zmap__step--home" for={`${id}-z0`}>World</label>
    </div>
  </div>

  {
    filters.length > 0 && (
      <div class="zmap__filters" role="group" aria-label="Show on the map">
        {filters.map((f) => (
          <label class="zmap__filter">
            <input type="checkbox" id={`${id}-f-${f.key}`} checked={f.checked ?? true} />
            <span>{f.label}</span>
          </label>
        ))}
      </div>
    )
  }

  <slot />
</div>

<style set:html={rules} is:inline></style>

<script is:inline>
(() => {
  for (const map of document.querySelectorAll(".zmap:not([data-mouse])")) {
    map.dataset.mouse = "";
    const view = map.querySelector(".zmap__view");
    const canvas = map.querySelector(".zmap__canvas");
    const radios = [...map.querySelectorAll(".zmap__radio")];
    if (!view || !canvas || radios.length < 2) continue;
    const level = () => radios.findIndex((r) => r.checked);

    const zoomAt = (to, x, y) => {
      const before = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
      const fx = (x - before.left) / before.width;
      const fy = (y - before.top) / before.height;
      radios[to].checked = true;
      const after = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
      view.scrollTo({
        left: view.scrollLeft + after.left + fx * after.width - x,
        top: view.scrollTop + after.top + fy * after.height - y,
        behavior: "instant",
      });
    };

    let pending = 0;
    let last = -Infinity;
    view.addEventListener("wheel", (e) => {
      if (e.deltaY === 0 || Math.abs(e.deltaX) > Math.abs(e.deltaY)) return;
      const to = level() + (e.deltaY < 0 ? 1 : -1);
      if (to < 0 || to >= radios.length) return;
      e.preventDefault();
      const px = e.deltaY * (e.deltaMode === 1 ? 40 : e.deltaMode === 2 ? view.clientHeight : 1);
      pending = Math.sign(px) === Math.sign(pending) ? pending + px : px;
      if (Math.abs(pending) < 50 || e.timeStamp - last < 200) return;
      pending = 0;
      last = e.timeStamp;
      zoomAt(to, e.clientX, e.clientY);
    }, { passive: false });

    let drag = null;
    let dragged = false;
    const scrollable = () =>
      view.scrollWidth > view.clientWidth + 1 || view.scrollHeight > view.clientHeight + 1;
    view.addEventListener("pointerdown", (e) => {
      dragged = false;
      if (e.pointerType !== "mouse" || e.button !== 0 || !scrollable()) return;
      drag = { id: e.pointerId, x: e.clientX, y: e.clientY, left: view.scrollLeft, top: view.scrollTop, moving: false };
    });
    view.addEventListener("pointermove", (e) => {
      if (!drag || e.pointerId !== drag.id) return;
      const dx = e.clientX - drag.x;
      const dy = e.clientY - drag.y;
      if (!drag.moving) {
        if (Math.hypot(dx, dy) < 5) return;
        drag.moving = true;
        view.setPointerCapture(drag.id);
        map.classList.add("is-dragging");
      }
      view.scrollTo({ left: drag.left - dx, top: drag.top - dy, behavior: "instant" });
    });
    const release = (e) => {
      if (!drag || e.pointerId !== drag.id) return;
      dragged = drag.moving;
      drag = null;
      map.classList.remove("is-dragging");
    };
    view.addEventListener("pointerup", release);
    view.addEventListener("pointercancel", release);
    view.addEventListener("click", (e) => {
      if (!dragged) return;
      dragged = false;
      e.preventDefault();
      e.stopPropagation();
    }, true);
  }
})();
</script>

<style>
  .zmap {
    --zmap-h: min(74svh, 76vw, 720px);
    /* Marker scale. The markers are drawn in pixels and the map at the width
       of the frame, so on a phone the same pixel size covers four times as
       much of the world. */
    --zmap-ms: 1;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .zmap {
      --zmap-ms: 0.55;
    }
  }

  /* Off-screen rather than display:none, so the zoom stays operable from the
     keyboard: tab to the group and the arrow keys step through the levels. */
  .zmap__radio {
    position: absolute;
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* The positioned box the cards are laid out against. It is deliberately the
     parent of the scroller and not the scroller itself: see the header. */
  .zmap__frame {
    position: relative;
    /* The size container the canvas measures itself against. Here and not on
       the scroller, because containment makes an element the containing block
       of its positioned descendants, which would put the cards back inside
       the scroller and under its clip. */
    container-type: inline-size;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r);
    background: var(--bg-deep);
    overflow: clip;
  }

  .zmap__view {
    display: flex;
    block-size: var(--zmap-h);
    overflow: auto;
    overscroll-behavior: auto;
    scroll-snap-type: both mandatory;
    scrollbar-width: thin;
    scrollbar-color: var(--line) transparent;
  }

  .zmap__view:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
    outline-offset: -2px;
  }

  /* Contained, not covered: at the widest level the whole world is in view and
     nothing scrolls, which is what lets the snap lattice keep the centre. The
     auto margins centre the map while it is smaller than the frame and resolve
     to zero once it is larger, so no part of it is ever out of reach. */
  .zmap__canvas {
    --z: 1;
    display: grid;
    grid-template: 1fr / 1fr;
    flex: none;
    inline-size: calc(var(--z) * min(100cqi, 2 * var(--zmap-h)));
    aspect-ratio: 2 / 1;
    margin: auto;
  }

  .zmap__canvas > * {
    grid-area: 1 / 1;
  }

  .zmap__svg {
    display: block;
    inline-size: 100%;
    block-size: 100%;
  }

  .zmap__land {
    fill: var(--surface-2);
  }

  .zmap__borders {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--line);
    stroke-width: 0.75;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  .zmap__cols,
  .zmap__rows {
    display: grid;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .zmap__cols {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(768, 1fr);
  }

  .zmap__rows {
    grid-template-rows: repeat(384, 1fr);
  }

  .zmap__cols > i {
    scroll-snap-align: none center;
  }

  .zmap__rows > i {
    scroll-snap-align: center none;
  }

  /* Placed with margins against the canvas. A vertical percentage margin
     resolves against the containing block's WIDTH, and the canvas is exactly
     twice as wide as it is tall, so half of --y percent of the width is --y
     percent of the height. */
  .zmap__site {
    --dd: calc(var(--d) * var(--zmap-ms, 1));
    justify-self: start;
    align-self: start;
    inline-size: var(--dd);
    block-size: var(--dd);
    margin-inline-start: calc(var(--x) * 1% - var(--dd) / 2);
    margin-block-start: calc(var(--y) * 0.5% - var(--dd) / 2);
  }

  /* The ring is the whole magnitude and the disc inside it the part, so the
     two read as one quantity at two stages rather than as two hues. With
     closest-side, 100% of the gradient is the radius, so --f is a fraction of
     the diameter. The half-pixel step keeps the disc's edge crisp. */
  .zmap__dot {
    --dot: var(--accent);
    display: block;
    inline-size: 100%;
    block-size: 100%;
    padding: 0;
    border: 1.5px solid var(--dot);
    border-radius: 50%;
    background:
      radial-gradient(closest-side, var(--dot) calc(var(--f, 1) * 100%), transparent calc(var(--f, 1) * 100% + 0.5px)),
      rgb(9 17 32 / 0.45);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px rgb(9 17 32 / 0.7);
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: transform 0.15s var(--ease);
  }

  .zmap__dot.is-muted {
    --dot: var(--muted-2);
  }

  .zmap__site:hover .zmap__dot,
  .zmap__dot:focus-visible {
    --dot: var(--accent-ink);
    transform: scale(1.2);
  }

  /* The card. Without anchor positioning it sits in the frame's top corner,
     which is still beside whatever the pointer is on. With it, it attaches to
     the marker and flips to stay inside the frame. */
  .zmap__card {
    display: none;
    position: absolute;
    inset-block-start: 0.6rem;
    inset-inline-end: 0.6rem;
    inset-inline-start: auto;
    inset-block-end: auto;
    z-index: 3;
    inline-size: min(19rem, calc(100% - 1.2rem));
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0.8rem 0.95rem 0.85rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r);
    background: var(--surface-2);
    color: var(--ink);
    box-shadow: 0 16px 44px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.55);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* Every area is anchored at one edge of the marker and extends away from
     it, so the fallbacks can always find one that fits. A single keyword
     such as block-start spans the whole row and centres on the marker, and
     near the edge of a phone screen that pushed the card half off it. */
  @supports (position-area: block-start) {
    .zmap__card {
      inset: auto;
      position-area: block-start span-inline-end;
      position-try-fallbacks: block-start span-inline-start, block-end span-inline-end,
        block-end span-inline-start;
      margin: 0.45rem;
    }
  }

  .zmap__site:hover > .zmap__card:not(:popover-open),
  .zmap__dot:focus-visible + .zmap__card:not(:popover-open) {
    display: block;
  }

  /* Opened by a tap or click it becomes a real popover in the top layer: it
     can take the pointer, carries its link, and dismisses on a tap outside. */
  .zmap__card:popover-open {
    display: block;
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    block-size: fit-content;
    margin: auto;
    pointer-events: auto;
    inline-size: min(20rem, calc(100vw - 2rem));
  }

  @supports (position-area: block-start) {
    .zmap__card:popover-open {
      inset: auto;
      margin: 0.45rem;
    }
  }

  /* On a phone an opened card is a sheet along the bottom of the screen: the
     one place it can never cover the marker it describes or run off an edge.

     The fallbacks go too, not only the area. A tap shows the card by :hover
     first, anchored, and Chromium keeps the last fallback that fitted as long
     as it still fits, so with fallbacks left on, a tapped card kept the
     anchored position `end span-start` as a 229px column instead of becoming
     the sheet. Opened by script with no hover first, it was a sheet, which is
     how the sheet looked tested when it was not. */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .zmap__card:popover-open {
      position-area: none;
      position-try-fallbacks: none;
      inset: auto 0.75rem 0.75rem;
      inline-size: auto;
      margin: 0;
    }
  }

  .zmap__name {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .zmap__sub {
    display: block;
    margin-block: 0.1rem 0.5rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .zmap__rows-list {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
    gap: 0.12rem 0.75rem;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .zmap__row {
    display: contents;
  }

  .zmap__row dt {
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .zmap__row dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .zmap__more {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-block-start: 0.55rem;
  }

  .zmap__controls {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block-start: 0.6rem;
    inset-inline-start: 0.6rem;
